/-!
# Full reverse kernels in the Borel Thompson mover

An actor in a Borel edge whose commutator on the full moving cover has
order at most two fixes a hyperplane. Its permutation image is trivial or
generated by a transposition, hence lies in the plane radical. The literal
edge chart therefore puts the whole actor inside the proper Thompson mover.

For a trapped critical path with small forward and predecessor slices,
the residual-shift argument bounds the full reverse-kernel commutator by
two. The full kernel, not just a paired intersection, consequently belongs
to the neighboring mover. Quadraticity makes that mover centralize the
commutator. The opposite seed centralizes it as well. If the opposite mover
omits the starting core, their generation identity gives centrality under
the starting residual, in addition to the established predecessor residual.

In the central-generation branch a distinct mover necessarily omits the
starting core. Combining this fact with equal-mover endpoint control covers
all comparisons: the endpoint commutator lies in the starting seed, or the
full reverse kernel gives a noncentral order-two line fixed by both actual
residuals. The literal OriginalSylowExceptional applications retain the
original graph, both moving covers and the odd cubic kernel E/Q.

The second alternative is not excluded here. Residual centrality does not
supply containment in the starting seed or whole-core exhaustion. The
strict forward bounds and terminal distance exclusions remain necessary.
The internal imports share the existing exact cover and edge-chart proofs.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher, Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank two
(1993), the comparisons in (5.7)--(5.8), printed pp.919--920;
Aschbacher--Smith C.1.31--32, printed pp.135--136.
-/

